{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hxcv2GBARcVQWtgc7AxVfA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Report: Defer Round 0 D2H Synchronization\n2\t\n3\t## Description\n4\t\n5\tRound 0 of the constraint evaluation phase in the GPU prover processes each AIR instance sequentially, launching a zerocheck kernel and a logup kernel per AIR, each followed by a **blocking D2H transfer** (`to_host()` -> `cudaEventSynchronize`). At APC 300 with 623 AIR instances, this creates up to ~1,246 blocking synchronization points, causing the GPU to sit idle during CPU-side IDFT processing and the CPU to block waiting for each tiny kernel result (<1KB each).\n6\t\n7\tThe optimization restructures the per-AIR loop in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` into two phases:\n8\t- **Phase 1 (GPU launch)**: Iterate all AIRs, launch both kernels per AIR, collect GPU result buffers without any D2H transfers.\n9\t- **Phase 2 (CPU processing)**: Explicit `current_stream_sync()`, then D2H transfers + IDFT + polynomial construction.\n10\t\n11\tThis was expected to eliminate GPU pipeline stalls between kernel launches and allow CPU setup work for AIR[i+1] to overlap with GPU kernel execution for AIR[i].\n12\t\n13\t## Implementation\n14\t\n15\t**File modified**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n16\t\n17\tChanges:\n18\t1. Added `use openvm_cuda_common::stream::current_stream_sync;` import.\n19\t2. Defined a local `Round0Pending<F, EF>` struct to hold per-AIR metadata (trace_idx, air_idx, coset counts, omega_root, n, zc_result, logup_result) needed for Phase 2 processing.\n20\t3. Replaced the single per-AIR loop (original lines 730-880) with:\n21\t   - **Phase 1 loop**: Iterates all AIR instances, launches zerocheck and logup kernels, stores `DeviceBuffer` results and metadata in `pending: Vec<Round0Pending>`. Also collects `d_main_parts` into a separate vec to keep them alive.\n22\t   - **Explicit stream sync**: `current_stream_sync().map_err(...)` after all kernels are queued.\n23\t   - **Phase 2 loop**: Processes all results — `to_host()` calls (now fast since stream is already synced), IDFT, polynomial construction. Logic is identical to the original code.\n24\t4. Error conversion: `CudaError` from `current_stream_sync()` is mapped through `MemCopyError::from(CudaError)` -> `LogupZerocheckError::from(MemCopyError)`, using existing `#[from]` derives.\n25\t\n26\t**Deviations from plan**: None. The implementation follows the plan exactly.\n27\t\n28\t## Results\n29\t\n30\tAll measurements are averages of 2 runs (confirmed consistent to within ~3ms).\n31\t\n32\t### Round 0\n33\t\n34\t| Config | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n35\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n36\t| APC 0 | 178ms | 178ms | 175ms | -3ms (1.02x lower) | -3ms (1.02x lower) |\n37\t| APC 100 | 463ms | 466ms | 380ms | -83ms (1.22x lower) | -86ms (1.23x lower) |\n38\t| APC 300 | 663ms | 662ms | 569ms | -94ms (1.17x lower) | -93ms (1.16x lower) |\n39\t\n40\t### STARK (excl. trace)\n41\t\n42\t| Config | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n43\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n44\t| APC 0 | 2149ms | 2165ms | 2146ms | -3ms (1.00x lower) | -19ms (1.01x lower) |\n45\t| APC 100 | 2159ms | 2156ms | 2077ms | -82ms (1.04x lower) | -79ms (1.04x lower) |\n46\t| APC 300 | 2478ms | 2458ms | 2374ms | -104ms (1.04x lower) | -84ms (1.04x lower) |\n47\t\n48\t### Constraints (total)\n49\t\n50\t| Config | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n51\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n52\t| APC 0 | 1290ms | 1301ms | 1290ms | 0ms | -11ms |\n53\t| APC 100 | 1398ms | 1394ms | 1310ms | -88ms (1.07x lower) | -84ms (1.06x lower) |\n54\t| APC 300 | 1663ms | 1641ms | 1542ms | -121ms (1.08x lower) | -99ms (1.06x lower) |\n55\t\n56\t### Scaling Ratio (APC 300 / APC 0 STARK excl trace)\n57\t\n58\t| | Baseline | Before | After |\n59\t|---|---|---|---|\n60\t| Ratio | 1.153 | 1.135 | 1.106 |\n61\t\n62\t### Correctness\n63\t\n64\tAll 94 `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass. Proof verification succeeds for all APC configurations (the benchmark verifies proofs as part of the recursion step).\n65\t\n66\t### GPU Memory\n67\t\n68\tPeak GPU memory (from prove output) unchanged at ~1.6 GiB for APC 300, same as baseline.\n69\t\n70\t## Assessment\n71\t\n72\tThe optimization **achieved a measurable improvement** but fell short of the primary success criteria:\n73\t\n74\t- **Primary target (STARK excl trace APC 300 -150ms)**: Achieved -84ms vs before task, -104ms vs baseline. **Not fully met**, though directionally positive.\n75\t- **Secondary target (Round 0 APC 300 -30%)**: Achieved -14% (662->569ms). **Not met** — the target was 30%.\n76\t- **Scaling ratio target (<1.10)**: Achieved 1.106, very close to the 1.10 target. **Mostly met**.\n77\t\n78\tThe optimization clearly works — Round 0 time for APC 300 dropped by 93ms, scaling proportionally with instance count (APC 100 saw -86ms, APC 0 saw negligible change as expected). The gap from the predicted ~200-300ms improvement is likely because:\n79\t\n80\t1. **CPU-side work in `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` already provided partial overlap**: The logup kernel launch function does significant CPU work (DAG construction, rule encoding, weight computation, H2D transfers for interaction data) before launching its kernel. Some of this CPU work was already overlapping with GPU execution in the original code, since the blocking sync comes *after* the kernel launch, not before the setup of the next iteration.\n81\t2. **The `cudaEventSynchronize` overhead itself is smaller than estimated**: The plan estimated ~200-300ms from eliminating 1,246 sync points. The actual overhead per sync point is likely <100us (hardware dependent), putting the total at ~60-120ms rather than 200-300ms — consistent with the observed 93ms improvement.\n82\t\n83\tDespite not meeting the ambitious targets, the optimization is clearly worth keeping:\n84\t- 84-104ms improvement on APC 300 STARK excl trace (3-4% reduction)\n85\t- Zero correctness risk (pure loop restructuring, no CUDA kernel changes)\n86\t- Minimal code complexity increase\n87\t- Improved scaling ratio (1.15 -> 1.11)\n88\t\n89\t## Future Work\n90\t\n91\t- **What worked well**: The two-phase approach cleanly separates GPU launch from CPU processing. The `Round0Pending` struct is a natural abstraction. The explicit `current_stream_sync()` makes the synchronization point visible and debuggable.\n92\t\n93\t- **Rayon parallelization of Phase 2**: Now that Phase 2 is isolated, the CPU-side IDFT processing is embarrassingly parallel (each AIR writes to distinct `batch_sp_poly` indices). Converting to `par_iter` could save an additional 30-50ms on APC 300 by parallelizing the 623 independent IDFTs. This is a natural follow-up.\n94\t\n95\t- **Multi-stream pipelining**: Use multiple CUDA streams to overlap Phase 1 GPU execution with Phase 2 CPU processing. While the deferred approach eliminated CPU-GPU interleaving overhead, it now serializes all GPU work before all CPU work. With 2-4 streams and careful synchronization, GPU kernels for later AIRs could execute while the CPU processes earlier results. Expected additional savings: 50-100ms.\n96\t\n97\t- **Apply the same pattern to other sumcheck rounds**: MLE rounds and other constraint evaluation phases may have similar per-AIR sequential D2H patterns. The two-phase approach could be applied there, though the savings would be smaller (fewer instances per round after folding).\n98\t\n99\t- **Batch stacked reduction degenerate kernels**: The next-highest-impact optimization target. Stacked Reduction at APC 300 is 309ms with ~10,348 tiny single-block kernel launches. Batching these into ~17 launches per round could save ~100-150ms.\n100\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c7f624f-3476-4fc9-b97f-853499dc45e9","uuid":"c7a19c0f-6308-49a7-9043-eaf814247c10","timestamp":"2026-04-10T18:32:34.557Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-10-1933-defer-round0-d2h-sync/report.md","content":"# Report: Defer Round 0 D2H Synchronization\n\n## Description\n\nRound 0 of the constraint evaluation phase in the GPU prover processes each AIR instance sequentially, launching a zerocheck kernel and a logup kernel per AIR, each followed by a **blocking D2H transfer** (`to_host()` -> `cudaEventSynchronize`). At APC 300 with 623 AIR instances, this creates up to ~1,246 blocking synchronization points, causing the GPU to sit idle during CPU-side IDFT processing and the CPU to block waiting for each tiny kernel result (<1KB each).\n\nThe optimization restructures the per-AIR loop in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` into two phases:\n- **Phase 1 (GPU launch)**: Iterate all AIRs, launch both kernels per AIR, collect GPU result buffers without any D2H transfers.\n- **Phase 2 (CPU processing)**: Explicit `current_stream_sync()`, then D2H transfers + IDFT + polynomial construction.\n\nThis was expected to eliminate GPU pipeline stalls between kernel launches and allow CPU setup work for AIR[i+1] to overlap with GPU kernel execution for AIR[i].\n\n## Implementation\n\n**File modified**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nChanges:\n1. Added `use openvm_cuda_common::stream::current_stream_sync;` import.\n2. Defined a local `Round0Pending<F, EF>` struct to hold per-AIR metadata (trace_idx, air_idx, coset counts, omega_root, n, zc_result, logup_result) needed for Phase 2 processing.\n3. Replaced the single per-AIR loop (original lines 730-880) with:\n   - **Phase 1 loop**: Iterates all AIR instances, launches zerocheck and logup kernels, stores `DeviceBuffer` results and metadata in `pending: Vec<Round0Pending>`. Also collects `d_main_parts` into a separate vec to keep them alive.\n   - **Explicit stream sync**: `current_stream_sync().map_err(...)` after all kernels are queued.\n   - **Phase 2 loop**: Processes all results — `to_host()` calls (now fast since stream is already synced), IDFT, polynomial construction. Logic is identical to the original code.\n4. Error conversion: `CudaError` from `current_stream_sync()` is mapped through `MemCopyError::from(CudaError)` -> `LogupZerocheckError::from(MemCopyError)`, using existing `#[from]` derives.\n\n**Deviations from plan**: None. The implementation follows the plan exactly.\n\n## Results\n\nAll measurements are averages of 2 runs (confirmed consistent to within ~3ms).\n\n### Round 0\n\n| Config | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| APC 0 | 178ms | 178ms | 175ms | -3ms (1.02x lower) | -3ms (1.02x lower) |\n| APC 100 | 463ms | 466ms | 380ms | -83ms (1.22x lower) | -86ms (1.23x lower) |\n| APC 300 | 663ms | 662ms | 569ms | -94ms (1.17x lower) | -93ms (1.16x lower) |\n\n### STARK (excl. trace)\n\n| Config | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| APC 0 | 2149ms | 2165ms | 2146ms | -3ms (1.00x lower) | -19ms (1.01x lower) |\n| APC 100 | 2159ms | 2156ms | 2077ms | -82ms (1.04x lower) | -79ms (1.04x lower) |\n| APC 300 | 2478ms | 2458ms | 2374ms | -104ms (1.04x lower) | -84ms (1.04x lower) |\n\n### Constraints (total)\n\n| Config | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| APC 0 | 1290ms | 1301ms | 1290ms | 0ms | -11ms |\n| APC 100 | 1398ms | 1394ms | 1310ms | -88ms (1.07x lower) | -84ms (1.06x lower) |\n| APC 300 | 1663ms | 1641ms | 1542ms | -121ms (1.08x lower) | -99ms (1.06x lower) |\n\n### Scaling Ratio (APC 300 / APC 0 STARK excl trace)\n\n| | Baseline | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ratio | 1.153 | 1.135 | 1.106 |\n\n### Correctness\n\nAll 94 `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass. Proof verification succeeds for all APC configurations (the benchmark verifies proofs as part of the recursion step).\n\n### GPU Memory\n\nPeak GPU memory (from prove output) unchanged at ~1.6 GiB for APC 300, same as baseline.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThe optimization **achieved a measurable improvement** but fell short of the primary success criteria:\n\n- **Primary target (STARK excl trace APC 300 -150ms)**: Achieved -84ms vs before task, -104ms vs baseline. **Not fully met**, though directionally positive.\n- **Secondary target (Round 0 APC 300 -30%)**: Achieved -14% (662->569ms). **Not met** — the target was 30%.\n- **Scaling ratio target (<1.10)**: Achieved 1.106, very close to the 1.10 target. **Mostly met**.\n\nThe optimization clearly works — Round 0 time for APC 300 dropped by 93ms, scaling proportionally with instance count (APC 100 saw -86ms, APC 0 saw negligible change as expected). The gap from the predicted ~200-300ms improvement is likely because:\n\n1. **CPU-side work in `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` already provided partial overlap**: The logup kernel launch function does significant CPU work (DAG construction, rule encoding, weight computation, H2D transfers for interaction data) before launching its kernel. Some of this CPU work was already overlapping with GPU execution in the original code, since the blocking sync comes *after* the kernel launch, not before the setup of the next iteration.\n2. **The `cudaEventSynchronize` overhead itself is smaller than estimated**: The plan estimated ~200-300ms from eliminating 1,246 sync points. The actual overhead per sync point is likely <100us (hardware dependent), putting the total at ~60-120ms rather than 200-300ms — consistent with the observed 93ms improvement.\n\nDespite not meeting the ambitious targets, the optimization is clearly worth keeping:\n- 84-104ms improvement on APC 300 STARK excl trace (3-4% reduction)\n- Zero correctness risk (pure loop restructuring, no CUDA kernel changes)\n- Minimal code complexity increase\n- Improved scaling ratio (1.15 -> 1.11)\n\n## Future Work\n\n- **What worked well**: The two-phase approach cleanly separates GPU launch from CPU processing. The `Round0Pending` struct is a natural abstraction. The explicit `current_stream_sync()` makes the synchronization point visible and debuggable.\n\n- **Rayon parallelization of Phase 2**: Now that Phase 2 is isolated, the CPU-side IDFT processing is embarrassingly parallel (each AIR writes to distinct `batch_sp_poly` indices). Converting to `par_iter` could save an additional 30-50ms on APC 300 by parallelizing the 623 independent IDFTs. This is a natural follow-up.\n\n- **Multi-stream pipelining**: Use multiple CUDA streams to overlap Phase 1 GPU execution with Phase 2 CPU processing. While the deferred approach eliminated CPU-GPU interleaving overhead, it now serializes all GPU work before all CPU work. With 2-4 streams and careful synchronization, GPU kernels for later AIRs could execute while the CPU processes earlier results. Expected additional savings: 50-100ms.\n\n- **Apply the same pattern to other sumcheck rounds**: MLE rounds and other constraint evaluation phases may have similar per-AIR sequential D2H patterns. The two-phase approach could be applied there, though the savings would be smaller (fewer instances per round after folding).\n\n- **Batch stacked reduction degenerate kernels**: The next-highest-impact optimization target. Stacked Reduction at APC 300 is 309ms with ~10,348 tiny single-block kernel launches. Batching these into ~17 launches per round could save ~100-150ms.\n","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":100}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FKkMQQpMVmg7e76oxFQzcK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RHmxKq4RMRxY1CbceqyAEf","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-10-2145-batch-degenerate-stacked-reduction/report.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4048,"cache_read_input_tokens":17537,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4048},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c7f624f-3476-4fc9-b97f-853499dc45e9","uuid":"07662eca-992f-4ecd-90ec-601851c88313"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RHmxKq4RMRxY1CbceqyAEf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Report: Batch Degenerate Stacked Reduction Kernel Launches\n2\t\n3\t## Description\n4\t\n5\tThe Stacked Reduction component of the GPU prover scales poorly with APC count: 113ms at APC 0 vs 312ms at APC 300 (2.76x increase), despite total work decreasing by >2x. The root cause is that `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` launches a separate CUDA kernel per \"window\" (one per trace matrix). For the degenerate case (`log_height < l_skip + round`), this produces ~10,348 single-block kernel launches at APC 300, each involving a fill_zero, H2D copy, kernel launch, and D2H sync. Nsight profiling confirmed ~70% of wall-clock time was CPU-side launch/sync overhead.\n6\t\n7\tThe optimization batches all degenerate windows into a single kernel launch per round, using one block per window, all atomically accumulating into a shared output buffer.\n8\t\n9\t## Implementation\n10\t\n11\t### Files changed\n12\t\n13\t| File | Change |\n14\t|---|---|\n15\t| `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` | Added `DegenerateWindowMeta` struct; restructured `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval` into 3 phases (classify, batched degenerate, per-window non-degenerate); removed unused `from_fn` import |\n16\t| `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu` | Added `DegenerateWindowMeta` struct; added `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_batched_kernel` and its launcher |\n17\t| `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs` | Added FFI extern declaration and safe wrapper for the batched launcher |\n18\t| `crates/cuda-backend/src/error.rs` | Added `SumcheckMleRoundDegenerateBatched` error variant |\n19\t\n20\t### Key decisions\n21\t\n22\t- **Block size fixed at 256**: The original kernel used `min(window_len, 256)`. Fixing at 256 means some blocks have idle threads for small windows, but this is negligible (~3.9us avg GPU time per window) and avoids per-window block size tuning.\n23\t- **Per-window metadata via device buffer**: A `DegenerateWindowMeta` struct (48 bytes) carries per-window parameters (col_offset, eq_ub_offset, window_len, eq_r, k_rot_r). At APC 300, this is ~10K * 48B = ~480KB, negligible.\n24\t- **Concatenated eq_ub upload**: All degenerate windows' eq_ub values are gathered into one host Vec and uploaded in a single H2D copy, with per-window offsets in the metadata.\n25\t\n26\t### Deviations from plan\n27\t\n28\tNone. The implementation followed the plan exactly.\n29\t\n30\t## Results\n31\t\n32\t### Stacked Reduction (ms)\n33\t\n34\t| Config | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n35\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n36\t| APC 0   | 113 | 113 | 101 | -12ms (1.12x lower) | -12ms (1.12x lower) |\n37\t| APC 100 | 201 | 203 | 127 | -74ms (1.58x lower) | -76ms (1.60x lower) |\n38\t| APC 300 | 308 | 312 | 147 | -161ms (2.10x lower) | -165ms (2.12x lower) |\n39\t\n40\t### STARK excl trace (ms)\n41\t\n42\t| Config | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n43\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n44\t| APC 0   | 2149 | 2178 | 2147 | -2ms (1.00x lower) | -31ms (1.01x lower) |\n45\t| APC 100 | 2159 | 2080 | 2006 | -153ms (1.08x lower) | -74ms (1.04x lower) |\n46\t| APC 300 | 2478 | 2370 | 2195 | -283ms (1.13x lower) | -175ms (1.08x lower) |\n47\t\n48\t### Scaling ratio (APC 300 / APC 0)\n49\t\n50\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task |\n51\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|\n52\t| Stacked Reduction | 2.73x | 2.76x | 1.46x |\n53\t| STARK excl trace | 1.153x | 1.088x | 1.022x |\n54\t\n55\t### Correctness\n56\t\n57\t- All 94 `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass\n58\t- Proof verification succeeds for all APC configs (0, 100, 300)\n59\t- GPU memory unchanged (peak within 1% of before)\n60\t\n61\t## Assessment\n62\t\n63\tThis optimization achieved all success criteria:\n64\t\n65\t1. **Primary**: Stacked Reduction APC 300 dropped from 312ms to 147ms (53% reduction, target was >50%). **Met.**\n66\t2. **Secondary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 dropped from 2478ms baseline to 2195ms (11.4% reduction, target was >10%). **Met.**\n67\t3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 STARK excl trace ratio dropped from 1.153 (baseline) to 1.022 (target was <1.05). **Met.**\n68\t4. **Correctness**: All tests pass. **Met.**\n69\t\n70\tThe combined effect of the two optimizations (Round 0 deferred sync + Stacked Reduction batching) brought STARK excl trace APC 300 from 2478ms to 2195ms, a cumulative 283ms (11.4%) improvement. The scaling ratio improved from 1.153 to 1.022, meaning APC 300 now performs nearly on par with APC 0 for STARK computation.\n71\t\n72\tThe Stacked Reduction scaling ratio (APC 300 / APC 0) improved dramatically from 2.73x to 1.46x. The remaining gap (1.46x vs ideal 1.0x) is likely from non-degenerate windows (which still have per-window launches) and residual overhead from the larger number of non-degenerate windows at higher APC counts.\n73\t\n74\t## Future Work\n75\t\n76\t- **Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels**: The non-degenerate path still uses per-window launches. At APC 300, these are fewer in number and have larger grids, but batching them could save another 30-50ms.\n77\t- **Rayon parallelization of Round 0 Phase 2**: The CPU-side processing in Round 0 could be parallelized across traces for ~30-50ms savings at APC 300.\n78\t- **Multi-stream pipelining**: The degenerate and non-degenerate phases could potentially overlap on separate CUDA streams, though the benefit may be limited since the non-degenerate kernels are already larger.\n79\t- **Persistent metadata buffer**: Currently `d_metas` is allocated/freed each round. A persistent buffer sized to the max degenerate window count could save allocation overhead, though this is likely negligible.\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c7f624f-3476-4fc9-b97f-853499dc45e9","uuid":"7c46acba-d986-4603-a5e7-9e0dbd753883","timestamp":"2026-04-10T18:32:34.984Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-10-2145-batch-degenerate-stacked-reduction/report.md","content":"# Report: Batch Degenerate Stacked Reduction Kernel Launches\n\n## Description\n\nThe Stacked Reduction component of the GPU prover scales poorly with APC count: 113ms at APC 0 vs 312ms at APC 300 (2.76x increase), despite total work decreasing by >2x. The root cause is that `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` launches a separate CUDA kernel per \"window\" (one per trace matrix). For the degenerate case (`log_height < l_skip + round`), this produces ~10,348 single-block kernel launches at APC 300, each involving a fill_zero, H2D copy, kernel launch, and D2H sync. Nsight profiling confirmed ~70% of wall-clock time was CPU-side launch/sync overhead.\n\nThe optimization batches all degenerate windows into a single kernel launch per round, using one block per window, all atomically accumulating into a shared output buffer.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Files changed\n\n| File | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` | Added `DegenerateWindowMeta` struct; restructured `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval` into 3 phases (classify, batched degenerate, per-window non-degenerate); removed unused `from_fn` import |\n| `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu` | Added `DegenerateWindowMeta` struct; added `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_batched_kernel` and its launcher |\n| `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs` | Added FFI extern declaration and safe wrapper for the batched launcher |\n| `crates/cuda-backend/src/error.rs` | Added `SumcheckMleRoundDegenerateBatched` error variant |\n\n### Key decisions\n\n- **Block size fixed at 256**: The original kernel used `min(window_len, 256)`. Fixing at 256 means some blocks have idle threads for small windows, but this is negligible (~3.9us avg GPU time per window) and avoids per-window block size tuning.\n- **Per-window metadata via device buffer**: A `DegenerateWindowMeta` struct (48 bytes) carries per-window parameters (col_offset, eq_ub_offset, window_len, eq_r, k_rot_r). At APC 300, this is ~10K * 48B = ~480KB, negligible.\n- **Concatenated eq_ub upload**: All degenerate windows' eq_ub values are gathered into one host Vec and uploaded in a single H2D copy, with per-window offsets in the metadata.\n\n### Deviations from plan\n\nNone. The implementation followed the plan exactly.\n\n## Results\n\n### Stacked Reduction (ms)\n\n| Config | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| APC 0   | 113 | 113 | 101 | -12ms (1.12x lower) | -12ms (1.12x lower) |\n| APC 100 | 201 | 203 | 127 | -74ms (1.58x lower) | -76ms (1.60x lower) |\n| APC 300 | 308 | 312 | 147 | -161ms (2.10x lower) | -165ms (2.12x lower) |\n\n### STARK excl trace (ms)\n\n| Config | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| APC 0   | 2149 | 2178 | 2147 | -2ms (1.00x lower) | -31ms (1.01x lower) |\n| APC 100 | 2159 | 2080 | 2006 | -153ms (1.08x lower) | -74ms (1.04x lower) |\n| APC 300 | 2478 | 2370 | 2195 | -283ms (1.13x lower) | -175ms (1.08x lower) |\n\n### Scaling ratio (APC 300 / APC 0)\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|\n| Stacked Reduction | 2.73x | 2.76x | 1.46x |\n| STARK excl trace | 1.153x | 1.088x | 1.022x |\n\n### Correctness\n\n- All 94 `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass\n- Proof verification succeeds for all APC configs (0, 100, 300)\n- GPU memory unchanged (peak within 1% of before)\n\n## Assessment\n\nThis optimization achieved all success criteria:\n\n1. **Primary**: Stacked Reduction APC 300 dropped from 312ms to 147ms (53% reduction, target was >50%). **Met.**\n2. **Secondary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 dropped from 2478ms baseline to 2195ms (11.4% reduction, target was >10%). **Met.**\n3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 STARK excl trace ratio dropped from 1.153 (baseline) to 1.022 (target was <1.05). **Met.**\n4. **Correctness**: All tests pass. **Met.**\n\nThe combined effect of the two optimizations (Round 0 deferred sync + Stacked Reduction batching) brought STARK excl trace APC 300 from 2478ms to 2195ms, a cumulative 283ms (11.4%) improvement. The scaling ratio improved from 1.153 to 1.022, meaning APC 300 now performs nearly on par with APC 0 for STARK computation.\n\nThe Stacked Reduction scaling ratio (APC 300 / APC 0) improved dramatically from 2.73x to 1.46x. The remaining gap (1.46x vs ideal 1.0x) is likely from non-degenerate windows (which still have per-window launches) and residual overhead from the larger number of non-degenerate windows at higher APC counts.\n\n## Future Work\n\n- **Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels**: The non-degenerate path still uses per-window launches. At APC 300, these are fewer in number and have larger grids, but batching them could save another 30-50ms.\n- **Rayon parallelization of Round 0 Phase 2**: The CPU-side processing in Round 0 could be parallelized across traces for ~30-50ms savings at APC 300.\n- **Multi-stream pipelining**: The degenerate and non-degenerate phases could potentially overlap on separate CUDA streams, though the benefit may be limited since the non-degenerate kernels are already larger.\n- **Persistent metadata buffer**: Currently `d_metas` is allocated/freed each round. A persistent buffer sized to the max degenerate window count could save allocation overhead, though this is likely negligible.\n","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":80}}}
