d by Round 0 at 570ms and Trace Commit at 409ms, so I need to figure out which single optimization would give the biggest win at APC 300.\n\nFor Round 0 specifically, the 464ms of GPU kernel time breaks down into 539 zerocheck coset_parallel instances averaging 408us each (220ms total) and 396 logup coset_parallel instances averaging 493us each (195ms total) — these small kernels are the real problem, and batching them could be the key improvement.\n\nThe constraint count does drop significantly from 1.13B to 505M, so if Round 0 time scales with constraint instances, it should be faster at APC 0, but I'm seeing the opposite pattern and need to understand why the per-AIR work isn't scaling as expected. about what NTT work is being done...\n\nIn Round 0 of the sumcheck, for each AIR instance, the prover evaluates the constraint polynomials over a coset of the trace domain. This involves:\n1. NTT of the trace columns to get their evaluations over the coset\n2. Evaluating the constraint polynomials at these points\n3. Computing the zerocheck polynomial values\n\nThe NTT size is determined by the trace height of each AIR. At APC 300, each AIR has a smaller trace (fewer rows), so each NTT is smaller. But there are 6.3x more AIRs. If the NTT size is N, the work is O(N log N), so 6.3x more AIRs each with N/6.3 rows should have total work of 6.3 * (N/6.3) * log(N/6.3) / (N * log(N)) ≈ 1 * (log(N) - log(6.3)) / log(N). For typical N = 2^20 and 6.3 = 2^2.65, this is (20-2.65)/20 = 0.87, so about 87% of the original work. That doesn't explain the 3.3x increase.\n\nThe real culprit is likely that many small AIR instances have tiny traces (log_height = 5, 6, 7), where per-instance overhead like kernel launches and NTT setup dominates the actual computation. Looking at the kernel time distribution, the average zerocheck coset_parallel kernel takes 408us with a median of 274us, meaning many instances finish in under 300us — they're extremely small and overhead-bound.\n\nFor these t