d in a single launch\n- For APC 300 with ~300 AIRs, you get ~300 kernel launches of `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel`\n- Each thread in the block re-initializes `cells[0..CELLS-1] = 0` for row hashing (lines 35-37)\n- This is NOT wasteful per se—it's necessary initialization for each row hash computation\n- **However**: The 584 figure suggests this is being called 584 times **total across all threads/blocks**\n\n**Could it be cached/deduplicated?**\n- **NO, not feasibly.** Each AIR instance has a different trace height and width, so:\n  - Different `query_stride` (depends on trace height)\n  - Different `width` (number of columns)\n  - Different output buffer address\n- The zero-initialization is a **necessary per-row computation**, not a global constant. Caching would only save bytes of constant initialization, which is negligible compared to the actual Poseidon2 permutation work.\n\n**Per-AIR Structure**:\n```\nFor each AIR instance (300 total for APC 300):\n  └─ stacked_commit() called once\n     └─ MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash::<MH>() called once per AIR\n        └─ compress_rows() → poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes kernel launched\n           └─ Each thread block: initialize cells[CELLS] = 0, hash rows, build merkle tree\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. **`batch_expand_pad_wide_kernel` (11,997 calls in nsight)**\n\n**Location**: \n- CUDA kernel: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/matrix.cu`, lines 204-221\n- FFI wrapper: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/matrix.rs`, lines 238-253\n- Rust caller: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs`, line 175\n\n**What it does**:\nExpands a `height × width` column-major matrix to a `padded_height × width` column-major matrix by zero-padding.\n\n**Per-AIR Launch Structure** (lines 204-221 in matrix.cu):\n```cuda\n__global__ void batch_expand_pad_wide_kernel(\n    Fp *__restrict__ out,\n    const Fp *__restrict__ in,\n    const uint32_t width,\n    const uint32_t padded_height,\n    const uint32_t height\n) {\n    uint32_t row = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    uint32_t col = blockIdx.y + blockIdx.z * gridDim.y;\n    // 2D+Z grid indexing for many columns\n}\n```\n\n**When it's called** (stacked_pcs.rs:175):\n```rust\nfor (mat_idx, j, s) in &layout.sorted_cols {\n    let start = s.col_idx * padded_height + s.row_idx;\n    let trace = traces[*mat_idx];\n    let s_len = s.len(l_skip);\n    if s.log_height() >= l_skip {\n        // ... full column copy via memcpy\n    } else {\n        // ***HERE: batch_expand_pad_wide called for EACH strided column***\n        batch_expand_pad_wide(dst, src, trace.height() as u32, stride as u32, 1)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Why 11,997 launches for APC 300**:\n- The `StackedLayout` contains `sorted_cols` which is a flattened list of all (matrix_idx, col_idx, slice) tuples\n- For AIRs with `log_height < l_skip`, each column requires a separate `batch_expand_pad_wide` call\n- **Per-AIR, this depends on**:\n  - Number of columns in that AIR's trace\n  - Whether those columns have height < (1 << l_skip)\n  - How many slices each column is split into in the stacked layout\n\n**Rough breakdown** (APC 300 analysis):\n- ~300 AIRs\n- Average ~40 calls per AIR = 12,000 total ✓ (matches 11,997)\n\n**Could they be batched?**\n- **YES, partially.** The kernel uses 2D+Z grid indexing (gridDim.y × gridDim.z) to process multiple columns\n- **However**: The launcher (`_batch_expand_pad_wide` at line 339-352 in matrix.cu) shows it already batches columns:\n  ```cuda\n  auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(padded_height);\n  grid.y = std::min(width, MAX_GRID_DIM);  // Batch multiple columns per launch\n  grid.z = (width + grid.y - 1) / grid.y;\n  ```\n- **The 11,997 figure likely counts thread blocks, not kernel launches**. With nsight's granularity, each (blockIdx.x, blockIdx.y, blockIdx.z) tuple in a single kernel launch is counted as a separate \"kernel launch\" event\n- **True kernel launch count** is probably ~300 (one per batch of AIR columns), but the 11,997 is the sum of all grid dimensions across launches\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Overall Trace Commit Flow and Per-AIR Structure**\n\n**Main entry point**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/mod.rs`, lines 104-197\n\n**Per-AIR Loop Structure**:\n```rust\n// Line 119-125: Commit ALL common main traces together (NOT per-AIR)\nlet (common_main_commit, common_main_pcs_data) = {\n    let traces = ctx.common_main_traces()  // Collect from ALL AIRs\n        .map(|(_, trace)| trace)\n        .collect_vec();\n    self.device.commit(&traces)?  // ***SINGLE STACKED COMMIT***\n};\n\n// Line 131-144: Per-AIR loop - extract metadata only\nfor (air_id, trace_ctx) in &ctx.per_trace {  // ***PER-AIR LOOP***\n    trace_vdata[*air_id] = Some(TraceVData {\n        log_height,\n        cached_commitments: trace_ctx\n            .cached_mains\n            .iter()\n            .map(|cd| cd.commitment)  // Cached commits already exist\n            .collect(),\n    });\n}\n\n// Line 158-175: Per-AIR loop - transcript observation\nfor (trace_vdata, pvs, pk) in izip!(&trace_vdata, &public_values, &mpk.per_air) {\n    if let Some(cd) = &pk.preprocessed_data {\n        transcript.observe_commit(cd.commitment);  // Preprocessed trace already committed\n    }\n    for commit in &trace_vdata.cached_commitments {\n        transcript.observe_commit(*commit);  // Cached traces already committed\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Key insight**: \n- **Common main traces** are committed together in a single `stacked_commit()` call (lines 119-125)\n  - All traces from all AIRs are stacked into one matrix\n  - One Merkle tree built for the entire stacked matrix\n  - Returns **1 commitment** for all AIRs' common main traces combined\n\n- **Preprocessed traces** are committed ahead of time (before the per-AIR loop), and commitments are stored in `mpk.per_air[*air_idx].preprocessed_data.commitment`\n\n- **Cached main traces** are committed ahead of time and stored in `trace_ctx.cached_mains[i].commitment`\n\n**The 300 AIRs DO NOT each get their own separate trace commit**. Instead:\n```\nTrace Commit Summary:\n├─ 1 commit: all common_main traces (300 AIRs stacked together)\n├─ N commits: preprocessed traces (one per AIR that has preprocessing)\n└─ M commits: cached main traces (depends on AIR configuration)\n\nTotal visible commits: 1 + N + M (NOT 1 per AIR)\n```\n\n**Where batch_expand_pad_wide comes in**:\nDuring `stacked_commit()` → `rs_code_matrix()` → `stack_traces_into_expanded()` (line 175 in stacked_pcs.rs):\n- The stacked layout iterates over all columns from all AIRs\n- For strided columns (height < l_skip), one `batch_expand_pad_wide` per strided slice\n- **Per AIR, overhead scales with**: (# of strided columns) × (# of height tiers)\n\n---\n\n### 4. **`poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` Scaling**\n\n**Location**: `/home/jorge/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/merkle_tree.cu`, lines 16-79\n\n**How it's called**: \nFrom `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/merkle_tree.rs`, lines 52-68\n\n```rust\npub unsafe fn poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes(\n    out: &mut DeviceBuffer<Digest>,\n    matrix: &DeviceBuffer<F>,\n    width: usize,           // # of columns in this matrix\n    query_stride: usize,    // height / rows_per_query\n    log_rows_per_query: usize,\n) -> Result<(), CudaError> { ... }\n```\n\n**Scaling**:\n- **Called once per stacked matrix** during Merkle tree construction\n- For APC 300: **1 kernel launch** (all AIRs' common main traces stacked)\n- **Number of row hashes computed**: `query_stride` (depends on total stacked height)\n- **Scales with cells**: YES, indirectly through `width` parameter\n  - More columns → more data per row → more Poseidon2 permutation rounds\n  - But the kernel is memory-bound, not compute-bound\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Per-AIR Overhead Summary**\n\nFor **each present AIR instance**:\n\n1. **Preprocessing**: Load preprocessed trace (if any) — already committed before prover runs\n2. **Cached mains**: Load cached traces (if any) — already committed before prover runs  \n3. **Common main trace**: \n   - NOT committed per-AIR; all common mains stacked together\n   - Overhead: sorting + stacking layout computation\n4. **Per-AIR in Trace Commit Phase**:\n   - Zero: no additional Merkle tree per AIR\n   - All traces use a single stacked Merkle tree for the common main\n\n**Estimated per-AIR overhead in Trace Commit**:\n- ~1 layout computation\n- ~(# of strided columns) × `batch_expand_pad_wide` calls\n- ~0 additional Merkle operations (shared across all AIRs)\n\n---\n\n### Recommendations\n\n1. **For `calculate_zero_hash`**: This is NOT a bottleneck—the initialization is negligible compared to the Poseidon2 permutation work. No optimization needed.\n\n2. **For `batch_expand_pad_wide`**: \n   - The kernel already batches columns via multi-dimensional grids\n   - The 11,997 figure is likely block counts, not kernel launches\n   - To optimize: Consider fusing this with the subsequent NTT/MLE operations, or pre-allocating padded matrices to avoid the padding step entirely\n\n3. **For Trace Commit Phase**:\n   - The single stacked Merkle tree is efficient—no redundant per-AIR hashing\n   - Per-AIR overhead is minimal and dominated by layout computation + batch_expand_pad calls\n   - Consider caching the stacked layout if proving multiple times with the same circuit\n\n---\n\n### File Reference Guide\n\n| Concept | File Path | Lines |\n|---------|-----------|-------|\n| Trace Commit entry | `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/mod.rs` | 104-197 |\n| Per-AIR loop structure | `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/mod.rs` | 131-175 |\n| CommittedTraceData type | `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/types.rs` | 18-99 |\n| stacked_commit() | `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs` | 48-84 |\n| stack_traces_into_expanded(