— that's a massive 86ms of CPU overhead. Even with the batched degenerate optimization already in place, there's still significant CPU bottleneck in handling the non-degenerate cases. said Stacked Reduction went from 312ms to 147ms at APC 300. The degenerate windows were batched. The remaining 148ms includes non-degenerate windows which still have per-window kernel launches.\n\nLooking at the non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels:\n- `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel`: 22ms, 797 instances\n- `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_kernel`: 22ms, 5076 instances\n- `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`: 9ms, 797 instances\n- `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel`: 9ms, 797 instances\n\nThat's 797 + 5076 + 797 + 797 = 7467 kernel launches for non-degenerate stacked reduction. Plus the batched degenerate kernel.\n\nEach non-degenerate window still launches its own kernel chain, and the CPU overhead from dispatching 7467 tiny kernels is probably eating up around 86ms. I could apply the same batching strategy we used for degenerate windows to these non-degenerate kernels too—they're more complex with larger data and multi-block grids, but the batching pattern would work similarly. If we eliminated that CPU overhead and got non-degenerate stacked reduction down to just 62ms of GPU time, we'd gain roughly 86ms at APC 300. Though at APC 0, the wall clock is only 101ms total, so I need to check what the actual GPU kernel time looks like there. ms, 319 instances\n- stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round: 15ms, 2454 instances\n- stacked_reduction_fold_ple: 15ms, 319 instances\n- fold_ple_from_evals: 16ms, 319 instances\n- Total GPU: ~85ms (wall clock: 101ms — 16ms CPU overhead)\n\nAt APC 0, the CPU overhead is only 16ms. At APC 300, it's 86ms. The increase is from 16ms to 86ms = +70ms. This is all per-instance launch overhead scaling with the number of windows.\n\nSo batching non-degenerate stacked reduction could save ~70ms at APC 300 (bringing it from 148ms to ~78ms). This would improve the APC scaling.\n\nNow I'm weighing the different optimization opportunities. Rayon-parallelizing Round 0 Phase 2 looks promising—it could save 60-90ms at APC 300 with high probability and simple implementation. Batching non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels is also strong, potentially saving 70-86ms, though it's more complex since these kernels are harder to batch than the degenerate ones. Batching the Round 0 coset_parallel kernels could save 50-100ms but has medium probability and higher complexity.\n\nThe LogUp GKR CPU overhead is harder to pin down—130ms is a lot but I'm not sure exactly where it's coming from. Multi-stream pipelining could help with 50-100ms savings but requires moderate-to-high complexity.\n\nLet me reconsider the Phase 2 work more carefully. The D2H transfer is very fast after the GPU sync, so the real bottleneck is likely the IDFT computation per AIR instance.\n\nLooking at the actual wall clock timing, if Round 0 takes 570ms total and the GPU kernels account for roughly 560ms, that leaves only about 10ms for Phase 2 CPU work—which seems inconsistent with my earlier estimates. The issue is that GPU kernels on a single stream execute sequentially, so the wall time should equal the sum of all kernel times, but something's not adding up here.\n\nWith the deferred sync optimization, Phase 1 launches all 935+ kernels before any synchronization, and the CPU dispatch overhead for these small coset_parallel kernels (averaging 408us each) is probably around 10-50us per kernel, totaling roughly 28ms. Since CPU dispatch and GPU execution overlap, Phase 1's wall time is dominated by the GPU's 560ms. Phase 2 then begins after the sync completes.\n\nPhase 2 involves 623 D2H transfers, IDFTs on small matrices, and polynomial constructions—if each IDFT takes about 10us, the whole phase runs in roughly 10ms, making rayon parallelization of Phase 2 not worth pursuing since it would only save a few milliseconds.\n\nThis means the real bottleneck is GPU kernel efficiency in Round 0. Since the kernels are already well-optimized and Phase 2 is negligible, the only meaningful improvements would come from either making the kernels themselves faster or pipelining GPU execution with other useful CPU work.\n\nThe core issue is that Round 0 has many small AIR instances running sequentially on the GPU, each with insufficient work to fully utilize the hardware. Batching multiple small instances into a single kernel could let the GPU process them in parallel and achieve better utilization.\n\nThe strategy mirrors the degenerate stacked reduction approach: classify AIRs as small or large, pack small AIR parameters into a metadata buffer, launch a single batched kernel where each thread block handles one AIR, and keep large AIRs on their existing per-AIR launches. This could potentially cut coset_parallel time from ~464ms down to 100-200ms, saving 260-360ms overall at APC 300, though the implementation is complex.\n\nThe constraint evaluation kernel is trickier than the stacked reduction case since each AIR has different bytecode-encoded constraints, interaction rules, trace layouts, and NTT sizes. But since the kernel already handles variable inputs through bytecode encoding, the main hurdle is managing per-AIR metadata—trace column pointers, public values, and constraint bytecodes—which would need to be packed as offsets into shared buffers for the batched kernel to access.\n\nThe bytecodes themselves are variable-length sequences, which complicates batching, but I can work around this by storing pointers to per-AIR bytecodes in the metadata. Each thread block would independently grab its AIR's metadata, load the corresponding trace data from the GPU, execute the bytecode-driven constraint evaluation, and write results to its own output slot—keeping thread blocks completely isolated from each other.\n\nThis approach mirrors the stacked reduction batching pattern and could cut coset_parallel GPU time from around 464ms down to 150-200ms, which would shave 270-320ms off Round 0 overall. The probability feels medium since it's more involved than stacked reduction but follows a proven approach. Now I'm reconsidering whether the improvement would actually be that substantial—I need to think through GPU utilization more carefully, especially for APC 300 where coset_parallel kernels handle instances with grid sizes capped at around 128 thread blocks.\n\nThe issue is that small AIRs might only spawn 1-4 blocks, leaving most of the GPU's 108-144 SMs idle. Batching 100 small AIRs into a single kernel would pack roughly 100 blocks and activate far more SMs simultaneously, which should significantly boost throughput. But I should verify this against actual kernel grid sizes for typical small AIRs at APC 300, where log_height ranges from 6-10 and I need to calculate num_x based on the specific round parameters.\n\nLooking at the coset_parallel kernel specifics, for a 64-row AIR with 3 cosets, the grid only has 3 blocks total with 192 active threads out of 768—most threads sitting idle. Even scaling up to 1024 rows would still underutilize the GPU significantly.\n\nThe key insight is that batching 100+ small AIR evaluations could theoretically achieve 10-50x speedup by filling the GPU's 108+ SMs, but that's overly optimistic since the actual bottleneck comes down to the total thread block count and memory constraints.\n\nLooking at the realistic numbers, if I focus on batching just the tiny kernels (under 4 blocks), I could save roughly 170-270ms from the coset_parallel portion. That would bring Round 0 down from 570ms to around 300-400ms total.\n\nNow I'm wondering if there's a simpler optimization approach I'm missing.\n\nThe batching idea for Round 0 zerocheck and logup kernels is the most impactful—potentially saving 150-300ms—but it's also quite complex. The constraint evaluation kernel varies per AIR with different bytecode, so I'd need to pack metadata carefully and handle heterogeneous workloads. A simpler alternative like Rayon-parallelizing Phase 2 would only save 30-50ms, which feels incremental compared to the complexity I've already tackled.\n\nActually, I'm realizing CUDA graph capture might sidestep the kernel complexity entirely. Instead of rewriting the constraint evaluation kernel to handle batching, I could capture all the Round 0 launches into a graph and replay it—that eliminates CPU launch overhead without touching the kernel code itself. Though CUDA graphs work best for repeated operations, and Round 0 only runs once per proof, so this might not be the right fit.\n\nThe real bottleneck isn't launch overhead anyway—it's that each coset_parallel kernel only uses a handful of blocks on a 108-SM GPU, leaving most of the hardware idle between launches. CUDA graphs won't solve that underutilization problem.\n\nLooking at the actual Round 0 timing data at APC 0, the larger non-coset-parallel kernels like zerocheck and logup_r0 are dominating the runtime—they're the ones fully utilizing the GPU. The coset_parallel kernels are fast individually but numerous, and batching them together could potentially unlock 5-10x better throughput for that specific kernel family.\n\nAt APC 300, the picture flips dramatically—coset_parallel kernels balloon to 464ms out of 560ms total (83% of Round 0 GPU time), compared to just 35% at APC 0. This shift makes batching coset_parallel kernels the clear optimization target, since that's where the time is actually spent at higher APC values.\n\nI'm going to pursue this as the primary optimization. Let me think through what other alternatives are worth mentioning.\n\nActually, I should reconsider the scope here. Looking at the full landscape of kernel candidates—the coset_parallel kernels at 464ms, the non-degenerate stacked reduction at 62ms, MLE round kernels around 70ms, and LogUp GKR kernels at 9ms—I need to pick the optimization that delivers the most impact relative to complexity.\n\nThe LogUp GKR evaluate_interactions kernel is consuming 427ms across 791 instances, which rivals Round 0 in total time. But there's another angle worth exploring: what if I could overlap Round 0's GPU execution with LogUp GKR computation using multi-stream scheduling? That might give me similar savings without the batching complexity of either kernel family.\n\nThe challenge is that Round 0 produces the sumcheck polynomials needed downstream, and the random challenge squeeze happens after Round 0 completes for all AIRs, so I can't truly overlap Round 0 with later rounds. But within Round 0 itself, I could launch multiple AIR evaluations concurrently on separate CUDA streams to maximize GPU utilization. That's really just another form of parallelism across AIRs, similar to batching but using the GPU scheduler instead of kernel fusion.\n\nMulti-stream is simpler to implement than kernel batching since it doesn't require modifying the actual CUDA kernels—I'd just assign each AIR to its own stream and let the GPU interleave the thread blocks. It won't be quite as efficient as true kernel batching due to per-kernel launch overhead, but I'd still expect to recover maybe 30-50% of the batching gains, which could save 100-200ms at APC 300.\n\nThe catch is that multi-stream introduces its own complications: separate memory allocations per stream, synchronization overhead, and the fact that the current codebase relies on a single CUDA stream abstraction. Refactoring the memory management and stream handling infrastructure to support this would be non-trivial.\n\nGiven these tradeoffs, I'm going to focus on batching the Round 0 zerocheck and logup kernels for small AIR instances instead. It directly addresses the biggest bottleneck—Round 0 takes 570ms and accounts for most of the scaling gap—and follows the same optimization pattern that worked well for the stacked reduction. I'm estimating this could save 150-300ms at APC 300.\n\nLet me verify the GPU utilization math before finalizing the task. At APC 300, the coset_parallel zerocheck kernel runs 539 instances in 220ms total, which works out to about 408 microseconds per instance. With only 3-12 blocks per instance on a 108 SM GPU running sequentially, that's just 3-11% occupancy per kernel—plenty of room to improve by batching.\n\nIf I could achieve full utilization, that 220ms could theoretically drop to 7-24ms, or more realistically 30-50ms accounting for overhead. The same logic applies to logup coset_parallel at 195ms. So batching the coset_parallel kernels alone could save 200-350ms conservatively, assuming 2-4x speedup. I should also check whether the non-coset-parallel kernels at APC 300 (~100ms estimated) are worth optimizing separately.\n\nActually, looking back at the previous report's recommendations—Rayon parallelization, batch stacked reduction, multi-stream pipelining—none of them specifically called out batching Round 0 kernels. But the profiling data I'm seeing now makes a strong case for prioritizing that instead.\n\nLet me reconsider what gives the best return on effort. The Round 0 coset_parallel kernels are consuming 464ms at APC 300 and appear to be underutilizing the GPU, with a proven batching pattern from stacked reduction that could save 150-300ms. That's solid, but I'm also noticing the LogUp GKR `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` is taking significant time—374ms for the true variant across 486 instances. I should compare these options more carefully to pick the highest-impact target.\n\nLooking at the GKR kernel metrics across both APC settings, there's an interesting pattern where the true and false variants swap dominance depending on the APC value. The combined GKR evaluation time jumps from 138ms at APC 0 to 427ms at APC 300—a 3.09x increase—which suggests this could be another high-impact optimization target worth exploring.\n\nSince the GKR input evaluation handles independent AIR interactions, batching these kernels could potentially cut the 427ms down to 100-150ms, saving 277-327ms overall. That's actually more significant than the Round 0 optimization. But I need to verify whether the kernel already has large grid sizes that would limit batching benefits, especially at APC 300 where the per-instance GPU time is already increasing.\n\nLooking at the grid sizes more carefully, the evaluate_interactions_gkr kernels have moderate occupancy (10-50%), so batching would help but probably not as dramatically as the tiny coset_parallel kernels. I should focus on the single optimization with the highest impact-to-complexity ratio.\n\nRanking my options: batching Round 0 coset_parallel kernels offers the biggest savings (150-300ms) but is more complex, while batching the GKR input evaluation kernels is simpler to implement since they all use the same kernel and already handle variable-length data. The Round 0 kernels are trickier because they're heterogeneous and have different grid configurations. I'm leaning toward the GKR batching approach since the implementation is more straightforward and the savings are still substantial (100-250ms). Looking at the performance impact, Round 0 batching saves more overall time and directly fixes the worst scaling issue, so that's the better choice. I'll batch the Round 0 coset_parallel kernels for zerocheck and logup on smaller AIRs, and now I need to create the task with a proper timestamp and start examining the batch_mle.rs file to understand the implementation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":472,"cache_read_input_tokens":82468,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":472},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c7f624f-3476-4fc9-b97f-853499dc45e9","uuid":"51d62a40-7fdc-4e1e-a46c-862ef62dc7e0"}
