{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015yecYeoLPW27JK41CTrWAd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the issue. The `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` function does significant CPU work per AIR:\n- Creates a DAG builder\n- Adds each interaction expression to the DAG\n- Sorts and deduplicates DAG indices\n- Creates SymbolicRulesGpu (encodes DAG into GPU-compatible bytecode)\n- Uploads bytecodes and weights to GPU\n\nThis is the CPU-side work that happens for EACH AIR instance in Phase 1. At APC 300 with 623 AIR instances, this is repeated 623 times. The DAG building + bytecode encoding + H2D uploads are serial CPU work.\n\n\nThe CPU preparation work for each AIR overlaps with GPU kernel execution from previous AIRs, so it doesn't necessarily add to wall time unless it becomes the bottleneck. Looking at the metrics, the CPU dispatch and preparation at APC 300 appears faster than the GPU execution itself, suggesting the GPU is the limiting factor rather than CPU preparation.\n\nHowever, at higher APC counts or with smaller AIRs, the CPU work could become problematic. The bottleneck would likely be in DAG construction and bytecode encoding, which scales with the number of interactions and expressions. Since this happens 623 times during proving, precomputing these bytecodes during keygen and storing them with the proving key could eliminate a significant portion of the Phase 1 CPU work from the hot path.\n\nBut the real issue is that this CPU work overlaps with GPU execution anyway, so removing it wouldn't actually improve the wall-clock time for Round 0 since the GPU is the limiting factor. Let me refocus on the batching opportunity—the key insight is that small kernels are underutilizing the GPU. Looking at the profiling data, I'm seeing the coset parallel kernels have highly variable execution times, with many instances running in just a few hundred microseconds and some as low as 5 microseconds, suggesting there's significant room for batching smaller AIRs together to improve occupancy. Looking at the coset_parallel path more carefully, I see that for the zerocheck kernel, num_cosets equals constraint_degree minus one, typically 1-3 for standard APCs. The logup kernel uses constraint_degree directly without the minus one, giving 2-4 cosets. With grid.x usually being 1 for small AIRs, this means we're looking at only 3-4 blocks total across the GPU—roughly 3-4% utilization on a modern processor, which is extremely inefficient.\n\nBatching 100 small AIRs together using grid.z could scale this to 300 blocks for zerocheck, dramatically improving utilization. The approach would use a 3D grid where z indexes into the batch, with each block loading its AIR-specific metadata and evaluating accordingly. But there's a fundamental problem: different AIRs have different sizes, so using max dimensions across all AIRs would leave blocks for smaller AIRs sitting idle.\n\nA cleaner solution is a 1D grid where metadata tells each block which AIR it belongs to and what work to do—similar to how the degenerate stacked reduction kernel operates. Alternatively, I could assign one or more blocks per AIR depending on its size, but that requires the kernel to track which AIR each block is processing and handle the coset dimension offset, which adds complexity. Let me think through what the zerocheck kernel actually needs to do: it reads constraint bytecodes and processes elements per coset...\n\nFor a batched version, each block would load its AIR's metadata (rules pointer, trace data, sizes), then run the same constraint evaluation and accumulation logic. The main challenge is that constraint bytecodes are variable-length per AIR, so each block needs its own rules pointer. This is doable but requires building out CUDA infrastructure: a metadata struct per AIR, uploading a metadata array to the GPU, and updating the kernel logic to use per-block metadata instead of global pointers.\n\nActually, before committing to a full batched kernel, let me think about whether there's a simpler path. Instead of rewriting the kernel, I could just use multiple CUDA streams to launch Round 0 kernels in parallel—the GPU scheduler would naturally interleave blocks from different AIRs, improving utilization without the complexity of a custom batched kernel.\n\nThe approach is straightforward: create a handful of streams, assign AIR instances round-robin, and launch kernels on their respective streams. This avoids writing new CUDA code entirely, though it does introduce complications around thread-safe buffer allocation and per-stream memory management. If we can push GPU utilization from 5% to 40% with 8 concurrent kernels, we're looking at a meaningful 2-4x speedup for that phase. But the current architecture uses a single global stream and shared temporary buffers, so there's some refactoring needed.\n\nLooking at how the constraint and interaction evaluation functions work, each call allocates its own temporary buffers. Running them concurrently on different streams means duplicating those allocations—roughly 40MB extra for 8 streams at 5MB each, which is manageable. The real friction is that the CUDA backend abstracts away stream management, so switching to multi-stream requires reworking that layer.\n\nGiven the refactoring cost and the fact that a batched kernel approach would eliminate per-kernel launch overhead entirely, I'm leaning toward the batched approach instead. Let me think through this once more to make sure I'm making the right call.\n\nI haven't actually measured GPU utilization directly, but I can reason about it from the timing. If Round 0 kernels take 560ms total and the wall clock shows 570ms, the GPU is running 98% of the time—but that's temporal utilization. Each individual kernel only lights up a small fraction of the SMs, maybe 3-10%, so spatial utilization is poor. The GPU is constantly busy launching tiny kernels rather than having many SMs working together on larger work. Batching would fix that by letting more SMs run in parallel on the same logical operation.\n\nLooking at the set_parallel kernels at APC 300, there are 935 instances across zerocheck, logup, and their variants, consuming 464ms total—about 496 microseconds per instance. Each one uses roughly 4-10 blocks, so with batching I could pack 10-27 instances together to fill the 108 SMs, resulting in around 47 batches. If I sort instances by size, the batches would be balanced and the GPU would do the same total work but with 10-20x better SM utilization, dramatically speeding up the smaller instances.\n\nThe large instances near the threshold already have decent utilization, so overall I'm looking at a 3-5x improvement for the coset_parallel portion—bringing 464ms down to around 116ms. That would push Round 0 from ~570ms to ~206ms total. Realistically with imperfect batching, I'm probably looking at 2-3x improvement instead, which gets me to around 286ms for Round 0—a savings of about 284ms.\n\nNow I'm going to write up the task.md with the current timestamp.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1192,"cache_read_input_tokens":95993,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1192},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c7f624f-3476-4fc9-b97f-853499dc45e9","uuid":"c2aa7b23-1583-459f-9f5c-577952ea7513"}
