{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LevzaHcezoYitytR2UiXWm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Task: Batch Degenerate Stacked Reduction Kernel Launches\n2\t\n3\tTask name: 2026-04-10-2145-batch-degenerate-stacked-reduction\n4\t\n5\t## Problem Statement\n6\t\n7\tThe Stacked Reduction component of the GPU prover scales poorly with APC count: 113ms at APC 0 vs 310ms at APC 300 (2.74x increase), despite total work (cells, constraints, bus messages) decreasing by >2x. This is the second-largest contributor to poor scaling after Round 0 (addressed in the previous task).\n8\t\n9\t**Root cause**: The `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` function in `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:793-912` iterates over \"windows\" (one per trace matrix), launching a separate kernel per window. For the degenerate case (`log_height < l_skip + round`), this produces **10,348 single-block kernel launches** at APC 300 (vs 3,281 at APC 0).\n10\t\n11\tEach degenerate window launch involves:\n12\t1. `d_accum.fill_zero()` — a separate memset kernel\n13\t2. H2D copy of `eq_ub_slice` (~1-2us)\n14\t3. Degenerate kernel launch with `grid(1), block(min(window_len, 256))` — avg 3.9us GPU time\n15\t4. `d_accum.to_host()` — D2H copy + implicit sync\n16\t\n17\tNsight profiling confirms: GPU kernel time for all stacked reduction kernels at APC 300 is ~93ms, but wall-clock is 310ms. **~217ms (70%) is CPU-side launch/sync overhead** from issuing 10,348+ tiny kernel launches sequentially.\n18\t\n19\tKey code locations:\n20\t- Rust loop: `stacked_reduction.rs:833-907` (the `for window in self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2)` loop)\n21\t- CUDA kernel: `stacked_reduction.cu:306-368` (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel`)\n22\t- CUDA launcher: `stacked_reduction.cu:537-570` (launches with `grid(1)`)\n23\t\n24\t## Proposed Approach\n25\t\n26\tBatch all degenerate windows within each call to `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` into a single kernel launch. This is safe because:\n27\t- Windows are independent (no data dependencies between them)\n28\t- All window results are summed at the end (line 909-911: `s_evals_batch.iter().map(|evals| evals[i]).sum()`)\n29\t- The kernel already uses atomic accumulation (`atomic_add_fpext_to_u64`)\n30\t- Overflow is not a risk: 10,348 blocks x ~2^31 max per component < 2^45 << 2^64\n31\t\n32\t**Implementation plan:**\n33\t\n34\t1. **New batched CUDA kernel** (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_batched_kernel`):\n35\t   - Grid: `(num_degenerate_windows)` — one block per window\n36\t   - Each block loads per-window metadata from a device array: window offset into `unstacked_cols`/`lambda_pows`, window length, `eq_r`, `k_rot_r`\n37\t   - Per-thread computation is identical to the current kernel\n38\t   - All blocks atomically add to a single shared accumulator\n39\t\n40\t2. **Rust-side changes** in `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()`:\n41\t   - First pass: separate degenerate vs non-degenerate windows\n42\t   - Degenerate path: batch all eq_ub values into one H2D copy, build per-window metadata, launch single batched kernel, single D2H copy\n43\t   - Non-degenerate path: keep existing per-window launch (these are larger and already auto-tuned)\n44\t\n45\t3. **Data layout for batched kernel**:\n46\t   - `unstacked_cols` and `lambda_pows` are already contiguous on device — just pass window offsets\n47\t   - Pre-upload concatenated `eq_ub` values in one H2D transfer\n48\t   - Per-window metadata struct: `{ offset: u32, len: u32, eq_r: FpExt, k_rot_r: FpExt }`\n49\t\n50\t## Expected Outcome\n51\t\n52\t- **Stacked Reduction APC 300**: ~310ms -> ~110-130ms (save ~180-200ms)\n53\t  - Eliminate ~10,348 kernel launches -> 1 launch per round (save ~100-150ms of CPU launch overhead)\n54\t  - Eliminate ~10,348 fill_zero calls -> 1 memset (save ~10ms)\n55\t  - Eliminate ~10,348 D2H copies -> 1 D2H (save ~10-20ms)\n56\t  - Eliminate ~10,348 H2D eq_ub copies -> 1 H2D (save ~10-20ms)\n57\t- **Stacked Reduction APC 0**: Minimal change (~113ms, only 3,281 launches — less overhead to begin with)\n58\t- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: ~2358ms -> ~2160-2180ms (cumulative with previous task: ~300ms vs baseline)\n59\t- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0): ~1.086 -> ~1.00-1.02 (near parity)\n60\t\n61\t## Success Criteria\n62\t\n63\t1. **Primary**: Stacked Reduction APC 300 drops below 150ms (>50% reduction from 310ms)\n64\t2. **Secondary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 drops below 2200ms (>10% reduction from baseline 2478ms)\n65\t3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 STARK excl trace ratio drops below 1.05\n66\t4. **Correctness**: All `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass; proof verification succeeds for all APC configs\n67\t\n68\t## Considered Alternatives\n69\t\n70\t1. **Rayon parallelization of Round 0 Phase 2** (~30-50ms savings at APC 300, High probability, Simple complexity). Chosen against because: lower absolute impact than Stacked Reduction batching. Good follow-up task.\n71\t\n72\t2. **Multi-stream pipelining for Round 0** (~50-100ms savings, Medium probability, Complex). Chosen against because: complex synchronization with uncertain overlap benefit. Higher risk.\n73\t\n74\t3. **Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels** (~30-50ms savings, Medium probability, Moderate complexity). Chosen against because: non-degenerate kernels already do more work per launch (auto-tuned grid.y), so per-launch overhead is proportionally smaller. Could be added as an enhancement within this task if the degenerate batching succeeds.\n75\t\n76\t4. **Batch MLE round kernel launches** (~20-30ms savings, Medium probability, Moderate complexity). Chosen against because: smaller absolute impact than Stacked Reduction. Less clear batching opportunity.\n77\t\n78\tStacked Reduction batching was selected because it has the highest (expected impact x probability / complexity): ~180ms savings with high confidence (windows are provably independent, all results are summed) and moderate implementation effort (new kernel + loop restructuring, following the proven pattern from the Round 0 optimization).\n79\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c7f624f-3476-4fc9-b97f-853499dc45e9","uuid":"b37b8bdb-7759-45c8-a785-8e6c209d9ee0","timestamp":"2026-04-10T18:52:02.515Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# Task: Batch Degenerate Stacked Reduction Kernel Launches\n\nTask name: 2026-04-10-2145-batch-degenerate-stacked-reduction\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nThe Stacked Reduction component of the GPU prover scales poorly with APC count: 113ms at APC 0 vs 310ms at APC 300 (2.74x increase), despite total work (cells, constraints, bus messages) decreasing by >2x. This is the second-largest contributor to poor scaling after Round 0 (addressed in the previous task).\n\n**Root cause**: The `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` function in `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs:793-912` iterates over \"windows\" (one per trace matrix), launching a separate kernel per window. For the degenerate case (`log_height < l_skip + round`), this produces **10,348 single-block kernel launches** at APC 300 (vs 3,281 at APC 0).\n\nEach degenerate window launch involves:\n1. `d_accum.fill_zero()` — a separate memset kernel\n2. H2D copy of `eq_ub_slice` (~1-2us)\n3. Degenerate kernel launch with `grid(1), block(min(window_len, 256))` — avg 3.9us GPU time\n4. `d_accum.to_host()` — D2H copy + implicit sync\n\nNsight profiling confirms: GPU kernel time for all stacked reduction kernels at APC 300 is ~93ms, but wall-clock is 310ms. **~217ms (70%) is CPU-side launch/sync overhead** from issuing 10,348+ tiny kernel launches sequentially.\n\nKey code locations:\n- Rust loop: `stacked_reduction.rs:833-907` (the `for window in self.ht_diff_idxs.windows(2)` loop)\n- CUDA kernel: `stacked_reduction.cu:306-368` (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_kernel`)\n- CUDA launcher: `stacked_reduction.cu:537-570` (launches with `grid(1)`)\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\nBatch all degenerate windows within each call to `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()` into a single kernel launch. This is safe because:\n- Windows are independent (no data dependencies between them)\n- All window results are summed at the end (line 909-911: `s_evals_batch.iter().map(|evals| evals[i]).sum()`)\n- The kernel already uses atomic accumulation (`atomic_add_fpext_to_u64`)\n- Overflow is not a risk: 10,348 blocks x ~2^31 max per component < 2^45 << 2^64\n\n**Implementation plan:**\n\n1. **New batched CUDA kernel** (`stacked_reduction_sumcheck_mle_round_degenerate_batched_kernel`):\n   - Grid: `(num_degenerate_windows)` — one block per window\n   - Each block loads per-window metadata from a device array: window offset into `unstacked_cols`/`lambda_pows`, window length, `eq_r`, `k_rot_r`\n   - Per-thread computation is identical to the current kernel\n   - All blocks atomically add to a single shared accumulator\n\n2. **Rust-side changes** in `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval()`:\n   - First pass: separate degenerate vs non-degenerate windows\n   - Degenerate path: batch all eq_ub values into one H2D copy, build per-window metadata, launch single batched kernel, single D2H copy\n   - Non-degenerate path: keep existing per-window launch (these are larger and already auto-tuned)\n\n3. **Data layout for batched kernel**:\n   - `unstacked_cols` and `lambda_pows` are already contiguous on device — just pass window offsets\n   - Pre-upload concatenated `eq_ub` values in one H2D transfer\n   - Per-window metadata struct: `{ offset: u32, len: u32, eq_r: FpExt, k_rot_r: FpExt }`\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n- **Stacked Reduction APC 300**: ~310ms -> ~110-130ms (save ~180-200ms)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 kernel launches -> 1 launch per round (save ~100-150ms of CPU launch overhead)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 fill_zero calls -> 1 memset (save ~10ms)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 D2H copies -> 1 D2H (save ~10-20ms)\n  - Eliminate ~10,348 H2D eq_ub copies -> 1 H2D (save ~10-20ms)\n- **Stacked Reduction APC 0**: Minimal change (~113ms, only 3,281 launches — less overhead to begin with)\n- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: ~2358ms -> ~2160-2180ms (cumulative with previous task: ~300ms vs baseline)\n- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0): ~1.086 -> ~1.00-1.02 (near parity)\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Primary**: Stacked Reduction APC 300 drops below 150ms (>50% reduction from 310ms)\n2. **Secondary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 drops below 2200ms (>10% reduction from baseline 2478ms)\n3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 STARK excl trace ratio drops below 1.05\n4. **Correctness**: All `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass; proof verification succeeds for all APC configs\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n1. **Rayon parallelization of Round 0 Phase 2** (~30-50ms savings at APC 300, High probability, Simple complexity). Chosen against because: lower absolute impact than Stacked Reduction batching. Good follow-up task.\n\n2. **Multi-stream pipelining for Round 0** (~50-100ms savings, Medium probability, Complex). Chosen against because: complex synchronization with uncertain overlap benefit. Higher risk.\n\n3. **Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels** (~30-50ms savings, Medium probability, Moderate complexity). Chosen against because: non-degenerate kernels already do more work per launch (auto-tuned grid.y), so per-launch overhead is proportionally smaller. Could be added as an enhancement within this task if the degenerate batching succeeds.\n\n4. **Batch MLE round kernel launches** (~20-30ms savings, Medium probability, Moderate complexity). Chosen against because: smaller absolute impact than Stacked Reduction. Less clear batching opportunity.\n\nStacked Reduction batching was selected because it has the highest (expected impact x probability / complexity): ~180ms savings with high confidence (windows are provably independent, all results are summed) and moderate implementation effort (new kernel + loop restructuring, following the proven pattern from the Round 0 optimization).\n","numLines":79,"startLine":1,"totalLines":79}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013uYrQKcZf3jUCovHjcb8wQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VQcGaDPDZ3pT3sXPVUE5wZ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# Task: Batch Round 0 Coset-Parallel Kernel Launches\n\nTask name: 2026-04-10-2049-batch-round0-coset-parallel\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nRound 0 of constraint evaluation is the worst-scaling component in STARK excl trace: 570ms at APC 300 vs 174ms at APC 0 (3.28x increase), despite total constraint instances *decreasing* by 2.23x. This inverse scaling accounts for +396ms of overhead and is the single largest contributor preventing STARK excl trace from scaling with workload reduction.\n\n**Root cause: GPU SM underutilization.** Nsight profiling shows Round 0 at APC 300 launches ~935 `coset_parallel` kernels (539 zerocheck + 396 logup), each processing a single small AIR instance. These kernels have tiny grids (3-12 thread blocks) on a GPU with 108+ SMs, resulting in 3-11% SM utilization per kernel. The kernels execute sequentially on a single CUDA stream, so the GPU processes them one after another, wasting 89-97% of its compute capacity on each launch.\n\nProfiling breakdown at APC 300:\n- `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<true,false>`: 220ms, 539 instances (avg 408us, median 275us)\n- `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<true,false>`: 195ms, 396 instances (avg 493us, median 378us)\n- `*_coset_parallel_kernel<false,false>` variants: 49ms combined, 535 instances\n- Total coset_parallel GPU time: **~464ms** (81% of Round 0's 570ms wall clock)\n- Non-coset-parallel kernels (larger AIRs): ~96ms\n- Phase 2 CPU processing: ~10ms\n\nAt APC 0, coset_parallel kernels total only ~60ms (137+small instances) because there are only 99 AIR instances with larger traces that better utilize the GPU.\n\nKey code locations:\n- Round 0 orchestration: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:599-928` (`sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`, Phase 1 loop at lines 744-841)\n- Per-AIR zerocheck wrapper: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0.rs:31-124` (`evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu`)\n- Per-AIR logup wrapper: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0.rs:132-275` (`evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu`)\n- CUDA zerocheck coset_parallel kernel: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/zerocheck_round0.cu:335-467`\n- CUDA logup coset_parallel kernel: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/logup_round0.cu:323-460`\n- Launcher dispatch (threshold check): `zerocheck_round0.cu:667-717`, `logup_round0.cu:658-810`\n- `COSET_PARALLEL_THRESHOLD = 32768` in both files\n- Existing batched reduction: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/sumcheck.cuh:261-291` (`batched_final_reduce_block_sums`)\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\nBatch multiple small AIR instances into single kernel launches using a per-AIR metadata approach. This follows the same pattern that successfully optimized Stacked Reduction (task 2026-04-10-2145), which achieved 2.1x improvement by batching ~10,348 degenerate window launches into single kernel launches.\n\n**Mechanism:** For each coset_parallel kernel (zerocheck and logup):\n\n1. **Classify** AIR instances into coset_parallel-eligible (small, `num_x * skip_domain < 32768`) vs non-coset-parallel (large), using the existing threshold check already in the launchers.\n\n2. **Create per-AIR metadata struct** containing: pointers to trace data (`main_parts`), constraint bytecodes (`d_rules`, `d_used_nodes`), selectors, eq values, public values, lambda powers, interaction weights; plus scalar parameters (`buffer_size`, `skip_domain`, `num_x`, `height`, `num_cosets`, `g_shift`). Upload metadata array to GPU via single H2D copy.\n\n3. **Launch batched kernel** with grid dimensions `(max_x_blocks, max_num_cosets, num_airs_in_batch)`. Each AIR's thread blocks are identified by `blockIdx.z`. Each block loads its AIR's metadata, bounds-checks (early return if `blockIdx.x >= this_air_x_blocks` or `blockIdx.y >= this_air_num_cosets`), then evaluates constraints exactly as the current single-AIR kernel does.\n\n4. **Batched reduction**: Use the existing `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` in `sumcheck.cuh:261-291`, which already supports segment offsets for multi-AIR reduction. Each AIR's partial sums are written to a contiguous region of a shared output buffer, with per-AIR offsets tracked in the segment array.\n\n5. **Non-coset-parallel AIRs** continue using the existing per-AIR kernel path (these are already well-utilizing the GPU with large grids).\n\n6. **Rust-side orchestration** in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` Phase 1: split the per-AIR iteration into two sub-phases — (a) collect metadata for small AIRs and issue a single batched launch, (b) issue individual launches for large AIRs. Phase 2 processing remains unchanged.\n\nThe batched kernel code would be structurally identical to the existing coset_parallel kernels, with the addition of `blockIdx.z` indexing into the metadata array and per-AIR bounds checking. Shared memory layout is unchanged (each block uses its own shared memory independently).\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n- **Round 0 APC 300**: Reduce from 570ms to ~250-350ms (150-300ms savings). The coset_parallel portion should improve 2-4x from better SM utilization — multiple small AIRs process concurrently across different SMs instead of sequentially.\n- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: Reduce from 2205ms to ~1905-2055ms (7-14% improvement vs current, 15-22% vs baseline 2478ms).\n- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0 STARK excl trace): Improve from 1.027 toward ~0.90 or lower.\n- **APC 0**: Negligible change (only 99 instances, most already use non-coset-parallel path with good GPU utilization).\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Primary**: Round 0 APC 300 wall time < 400ms (currently 570ms, target >30% reduction).\n2. **Secondary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 < 2050ms (currently 2205ms, target >7% reduction).\n3. **Scaling**: STARK excl trace APC 300 / APC 0 ratio < 0.97 (currently 1.027).\n4. **Correctness**: All 94 `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass. Proof verification succeeds for APC 0/100/300.\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n1. **Rayon-parallelize Round 0 Phase 2 CPU processing** (estimated ~10ms savings at APC 300). Phase 2 is only ~10ms wall time because the Phase 1 deferred-sync optimization (task 2026-04-10-1933) moved the bottleneck to GPU execution. Far too small to justify as a standalone task.\n\n2. **Multi-stream Round 0 (launch per-AIR kernels on N CUDA streams)** (estimated 100-200ms savings). Achieves better GPU utilization without new kernel code by letting the GPU scheduler interleave blocks from different streams. However, requires refactoring the single-stream architecture in `cuda-common`, per-stream temporary buffer allocation (higher memory), and still has per-kernel CPU launch overhead (~28ms for 935 launches). Batched kernel approach is more efficient, has lower memory overhead, and follows the proven pattern from stacked reduction.\n\n3. **Batch LogUp GKR input evaluation kernels** (estimated 100-250ms savings). The `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` totals 427ms at APC 300 with 791 instances and has similar SM underutilization. However, the GKR protocol has sequential layer dependencies that limit overall pipeline improvement, and the kernel's interaction data structures are more complex to batch. Round 0 coset_parallel has a larger absolute improvement potential (464ms target vs 427ms) and is simpler to batch since the existing `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` can be reused directly. GKR batching is a good follow-up target.\n\n4. **Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels** (estimated 70-86ms savings). The non-degenerate path still has 7467 per-window kernel launches contributing ~86ms of CPU overhead at APC 300. Moderate impact but smaller than Round 0 batching. Good follow-up target after this optimization.\n\n5. **Cache `calculate_zero_hash` results by trace height** (initially estimated ~100ms savings). Investigation revealed this kernel is in the openvm VM memory Merkle tree (`openvm/crates/vm/cuda/src/system/memory/merkle_tree.cu`), part of the Set Initial Memory phase — NOT within STARK excl trace. Out of scope.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2131,"cache_read_input_tokens":108750,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2131},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c7f624f-3476-4fc9-b97f-853499dc45e9","uuid":"96f1224f-21d9-4a2b-bc12-3033a45fedcf"}
